# M_PwModelExample_WeatheredWood - procedural wood grain, tinted BY vertex colour.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `MI_PwModelExample_MatteDielectric` is a flat dielectric
# reading a flat per-face vertex colour, and on `driftwood` that rendered as
# painted clay - the report was "material itself does not look like driftwood at
# all", and the before frame agrees: a smooth pale rod with tan slots that read as
# strips of tape. Vertex colour is flat per face BY CONSTRUCTION - the overlay
# stores one value per triangle corner - so it can carry which REGION a face
# belongs to and nothing finer. Grain, fibre and weathering are sub-face detail
# and need a graph. This is that graph.
#
# IT DOES NOT REPLACE VERTEX COLOUR, IT MODULATES IT. `M_PwModelExample_VertexColor`
# wires VertexColor straight into Base Color; this master keeps that node as the
# tone the whole surface is built on and multiplies grain, checks and weathering
# over it. So `driftwood`'s five per-limb body tints (0.30 on the trunk up to 0.50
# on the knob), its dark fissure tools and its honey heartwood all still read, and
# any other model that binds this master keeps whatever its `color=` ops said.
#
# HOW TO INSTALL IT.
#
#   call("material.compile_mgir", { text: <this file>, save: true })
#   call("material.authoring.compile_material",
#        { assetPath: "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/M_PwModelExample_WeatheredWood" })
#
# `compile_mgir` does NOT block on shader compilation and returns no compile
# errors, so the second call is where a broken graph actually surfaces - read
# `compileSucceeded`, not the first response's `blocksCompiled`.
#
# The defaults below are DRIFTWOOD's, because that is the model that forced this
# material and shipping a master nobody can bind is worse than shipping one whose
# defaults name a wood. Every one of them is a parameter; see THE WOOD TABLE at
# the bottom for oak, boxwood and rough timber.
#
# ###########################################################################
# THE TWO UV AXES DO NOT BEHAVE THE SAME WAY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK.
# A `.pwmodel` `uv mode=cylindrical` puts U around the limb and V along it, so
# anisotropic noise stretched in V runs WITH the grain. But the two axes carry
# COMPLETELY different ranges, and assuming they matched cost this material its
# first three renders - the grain came back as soft camouflage blotches with no
# fibre in them at all, because every bit of the visible variation was coming from
# V and U was contributing a constant.
#
#   U (around the limb) spans about 0..1 PER FULL TURN, whatever `scale=` says.
#     The projection frame's scale does not divide the angular coordinate.
#     `GrainAcross` is therefore READ DIRECTLY AS "fibres around the limb" and it
#     PORTS BETWEEN MODELS unchanged.
#
#   V (along the limb) is world distance DIVIDED by `scale=`. `driftwood` carries
#     `uv ... scale=(0.02, 0.02)`, so V runs at 50 UV units per world unit and a
#     475-long spar spans about 24,000. `GrainAlong` is in 1/UV and must be
#     RE-DERIVED per model:  GrainAlong = uvScale / streakLengthInWorldUnits.
#
# Measured, not assumed. The axial figure came from instancing
# `M_PwReview_UVChecker` down to `Tiles: 0.0004` until single bands were countable
# - about nine bands across the 475-long spar, i.e. one 2500-UV tile per ~50 world
# units. The angular figure came from setting `GrainAlong: 0` so the noise could
# only vary around the limb, then bracketing `GrainAcross`: at 0.45 the limb was a
# flat uniform tone, and at 24 it was a dense fibre. A range that needs a
# multiplier of 24 to show one feature per turn is a range of about 1.
#
# TWO TRAPS ON THAT PROCEDURE, both of which look like a broken material:
#   - At the checker's own default `Tiles: 8` the mesh renders a FLAT MID-GREY.
#     That is 190,000 tiles aliasing to the average of the two checker colours,
#     not a mesh with no UVs. Drop Tiles until you can count bands.
#   - `GrainAlong: 0` renders a uniform DARK limb, not a blank one, because the
#     grain multiplier collapses to its low end. Uniform is the signal; the
#     darkness is incidental.
#
# The 20:1-and-up ratio between the two axes IS the grain. Bring them together and
# you get isotropic noise, which reads as a lumpy sausage - the same failure
# `driftwood`'s own header records for `noise_deform`, in the material instead of
# the mesh.
# ###########################################################################
#
# WEATHERING IS DRIVEN BY THE VERTEX COLOUR'S SATURATION, WHICH IS THE ONE TRICK
# HERE WORTH READING TWICE. Sea-worn wood silvers; wood a fresh break just exposed
# stays warm. Those two states are already distinguishable in the data, because a
# bleached body tint is nearly neutral and a fresh-break tint is not:
#
#       trunk body      (0.30, 0.29, 0.265)   saturation 0.12  -> bleached
#       fissure tools   (0.19, 0.17, 0.135)   saturation 0.29  -> part bleached
#       break planes    (0.35, 0.30, 0.245)   saturation 0.30  -> part bleached
#       heartwood tool  (0.66, 0.53, 0.34)    saturation 0.48  -> left warm
#
# `Bleach` is therefore scaled per pixel by `1 - saturate(sat * FreshSensitivity)`.
# The body silvers, the fresh break keeps its honey, and the insides of the splits
# - cut by tools carrying the warmer tints - stay darker and warmer than the
# weathered flanks around them, which is where weathering does not reach on a real
# piece. It also means this master does not need a second slot to say "heartwood":
# `driftwood`'s materials block argues at length against inventing a second
# shading behaviour, and this keeps that argument intact.
#
# The escape hatch is `FreshSensitivity: 0`, which makes the mask constant 1 and
# bleaches everything uniformly. An oak or boxwood consumer instead sets
# `Bleach: 0` and never reaches the mask at all.
#
# THE NORMAL IS THE HALF THAT STOPS IT READING AS PAINT, and it is cheap: the
# grain noise is evaluated a second time one small step along U, and the
# difference is the slope across the fibre. `float3(dU * GrainRelief, 0, 1)`
# normalised is a valid tangent-space normal that tilts only across the grain,
# which is the only direction a longitudinal fibre HAS a slope in. Without it the
# grain is a stripe painted on a smooth revolve and the light does not catch it -
# and `driftwood` is a smooth revolve by construction, with nothing else to catch
# light.
#
# Three Noise evaluations is the whole cost: two for the grain (the second is the
# normal's finite difference) and one for the checks.
# `NOISEFUNCTION_GradientALU` is about 80 instructions per level, so at Levels 3,
# 3 and 2 this graph is roughly 640 ALU instructions of noise. That is a hero-prop
# budget, not a terrain budget. `NOISEFUNCTION_ValueALU` is about a third cheaper
# if a consumer needs it and will not notice the softer crests.
#
# WHY THE GEOMETRY'S OWN CORDUROY IS NOT COMPETITION. `driftwood`'s header records
# four authors hunting a fine corrugation that turned out to be a grid texture,
# and the correction it draws - weathered wood IS corrugated along the grain - is
# now geometry at wavelengths down to about 8 world units, the finest its mesh can
# carry. This material works BELOW that: at `GrainAcross: 13` the fibre is roughly
# thirteen ridges around a limb, which on a 12-unit radius is a ridge every 6
# world units and finer on the thin limbs. The two stack rather than beat.
#
# NO TEXTURE ASSET, deliberately, and for the same reason `M_PwReview_UVChecker`
# has none: `NOISEFUNCTION_GradientALU` is pure ALU Perlin with no sample, so
# there is no import step, no sRGB or compression setting to get wrong, no mip
# chain, and nothing for a future reader to mistake for a mesh defect. It also
# keeps `driftwood`'s standing rule - NO TEXTURED MATERIAL, EVER, ON THIS MODEL -
# true as written.

entry material `/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/M_PwModelExample_WeatheredWood` {
    property MaterialDomain: MD_Surface
    property BlendMode: BLEND_Opaque
    property ShadingModel: MSM_DefaultLit
    property TwoSided: false
    property bIsThinSurface: false
    property TranslucencyLightingMode: TLM_VolumetricNonDirectional
    property bUseMaterialAttributes: false
    property OpacityMaskClipValue: 0.333300
    property NumCustomizedUVs: 0

    # ---- constants ---------------------------------------------------------
    # `%uv.r` / `%uv.g` is MGIR's channel swizzle on an input reference.
    # TextureCoordinate has ONE output pin carrying a float2, so the index form
    # (`%uv[1]`) does not reach a channel here the way it does on VertexColor's
    # five separate output pins.
    %zero = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionConstant`(R: 0.0) @(-2600, 560)
    %one = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionConstant`(R: 1.0) @(-2600, 640)

    # The finite-difference step for the normal, in noise-space units. 0.18 of a
    # feature: large enough to survive float precision on a V that reaches ~24000,
    # small enough that the difference is still a slope and not a second pattern.
    %reliefStep = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionConstant`(R: 0.18) @(-2600, 720)

    %uv = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionTextureCoordinate`(CoordinateIndex: 0, UTiling: 1.0, VTiling: 1.0) @(-2600, 0)
    %vertexColor = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionVertexColor`() @(-2600, 200)

    # ---- parameters --------------------------------------------------------
    # GrainAcross is fibres per turn and ports between models; GrainAlong is
    # 1/UV and does not. See THE TWO UV AXES above before copying either.
    %grainAcross = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "GrainAcross", Group: "Grain", SortPriority: 0, DefaultValue: 13.0) @(-2600, -300)
    %grainAlong = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "GrainAlong", Group: "Grain", SortPriority: 1, DefaultValue: 0.0007) @(-2600, -220)
    %grainRidges = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "GrainRidges", Group: "Grain", SortPriority: 2, DefaultValue: 2.0) @(-2600, -140)
    %grainContrast = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "GrainContrast", Group: "Grain", SortPriority: 3, DefaultValue: 0.4) @(-2600, -60)
    %grainRelief = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "GrainRelief", Group: "Grain", SortPriority: 4, DefaultValue: 1.1) @(-2600, 20)

    %checkAcross = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "CheckAcross", Group: "Checks", SortPriority: 16, DefaultValue: 5.0) @(-2600, 100)
    %checkAlong = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "CheckAlong", Group: "Checks", SortPriority: 17, DefaultValue: 0.0001) @(-2600, 180)
    %checkSharpness = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "CheckSharpness", Group: "Checks", SortPriority: 18, DefaultValue: 6.0) @(-2600, 260)
    %checkDepth = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "CheckDepth", Group: "Checks", SortPriority: 19, DefaultValue: 0.55) @(-2600, 340)

    %bleach = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "Bleach", Group: "Weathering", SortPriority: 32, DefaultValue: 0.85) @(-2600, 420)
    %freshSensitivity = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "FreshSensitivity", Group: "Weathering", SortPriority: 33, DefaultValue: 3.2) @(-2600, 500)
    %silverTint = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionVectorParameter`(ParameterName: "SilverTint", Group: "Weathering", SortPriority: 34, DefaultValue: [0.95, 0.97, 1.05, 1.0]) @(-2600, 800)

    %roughnessMin = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "RoughnessMin", Group: "Surface", SortPriority: 48, DefaultValue: 0.76) @(-2600, 880)
    %roughnessMax = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "RoughnessMax", Group: "Surface", SortPriority: 49, DefaultValue: 0.98) @(-2600, 960)
    %metallic = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "Metallic", Group: "Surface", SortPriority: 50, DefaultValue: 0.0) @(-2600, 1040)
    %specular = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "Specular", Group: "Surface", SortPriority: 51, DefaultValue: 0.22) @(-2600, 1120)

    # ---- grain: anisotropic noise, stretched along V ------------------------
    %grainU = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %uv.r, B: %grainAcross) @(-2300, -260)
    %grainV = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %uv.g, B: %grainAlong) @(-2300, -180)
    %grainUV = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %grainU, B: %grainV) @(-2100, -220)
    %grainPos = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %grainUV, B: %zero) @(-1900, -220)
    %grainRaw = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionNoise`(World Position: %grainPos, NoiseFunction: "NOISEFUNCTION_GradientALU", Scale: 1.0, Quality: 2, Levels: 3, OutputMin: 0.0, OutputMax: 1.0, LevelScale: 2.2, bTurbulence: true, bTiling: false) @(-1700, -220)

    # Power skews a symmetric noise toward the dark end, which is what separates a
    # hard latewood band from the soft spring wood either side of it. At 1.0 the
    # grain is a soft cloud; the ridging is the difference between "wood" and "mud".
    %grain = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionPower`(Base: %grainRaw, Exponent: %grainRidges) @(-1500, -220)

    # ---- the same grain, one step along U, for the tangent-space normal -----
    %grainUStep = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAdd`(A: %grainU, B: %reliefStep) @(-2300, -100)
    %grainUVStep = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %grainUStep, B: %grainV) @(-2100, -100)
    %grainPosStep = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %grainUVStep, B: %zero) @(-1900, -100)
    %grainRawStep = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionNoise`(World Position: %grainPosStep, NoiseFunction: "NOISEFUNCTION_GradientALU", Scale: 1.0, Quality: 2, Levels: 3, OutputMin: 0.0, OutputMax: 1.0, LevelScale: 2.2, bTurbulence: true, bTiling: false) @(-1700, -100)
    %grainStep = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionPower`(Base: %grainRawStep, Exponent: %grainRidges) @(-1500, -100)

    %grainSlope = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionSubtract`(A: %grainStep, B: %grain) @(-1300, -160)
    %normalX = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %grainSlope, B: %grainRelief) @(-1100, -160)
    # Y is a hard zero: a fibre running along V has slope across it and none along
    # it, so tilting in Y would only add a cross-grain wobble the wood does not
    # have. The sign of X is not corrected either - on a symmetric noise an
    # inverted relief is the same relief.
    %normalXY = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %normalX, B: %zero) @(-900, -160)
    %normalXYZ = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %normalXY, B: %one) @(-700, -160)
    %normal = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionNormalize`(VectorInput: %normalXYZ) @(-500, -160)

    # ---- checks: sparse, very long splits running with the grain ------------
    %checkU = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %uv.r, B: %checkAcross) @(-2300, 60)
    %checkV = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %uv.g, B: %checkAlong) @(-2300, 140)
    %checkUV = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %checkU, B: %checkV) @(-2100, 100)
    %checkPos = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAppendVector`(A: %checkUV, B: %zero) @(-1900, 100)
    %checkRaw = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionNoise`(World Position: %checkPos, NoiseFunction: "NOISEFUNCTION_GradientALU", Scale: 1.0, Quality: 1, Levels: 2, OutputMin: 0.0, OutputMax: 1.0, LevelScale: 2.0, bTurbulence: true, bTiling: false) @(-1700, 100)

    # An exponent of 6 crushes everything but the crests, so what survives is a
    # thin line rather than a broad band. This is the cheap stand-in for the F2-F1
    # a Voronoi cell border would give: `material.authoring.add_voronoi` is this
    # same Noise class with NOISEFUNCTION_VoronoiALU, which returns distance to
    # the nearest feature point only - that makes blobs, not borders.
    %check = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionPower`(Base: %checkRaw, Exponent: %checkSharpness) @(-1500, 100)
    %checkFloor = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionOneMinus`(Input: %checkDepth) @(-1500, 200)
    %checkMul = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionLinearInterpolate`(A: %one, B: %checkFloor, Alpha: %check) @(-1300, 140)

    # ---- weathering mask from vertex-colour saturation ---------------------
    # RgbToHsv's G channel is saturation, which is one node where a max/min pair
    # over three masked channels would be seven.
    %hsv = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionRgbToHsv`(Input: %vertexColor) @(-2300, 260)
    %freshRaw = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %hsv.g, B: %freshSensitivity) @(-2100, 300)
    %fresh = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionSaturate`(Input: %freshRaw) @(-1900, 300)
    %weathered = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionOneMinus`(Input: %fresh) @(-1700, 300)
    %bleachLocal = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %bleach, B: %weathered) @(-1500, 300)

    # ---- base tone: desaturate toward silver, then modulate ----------------
    # Desaturation preserves luminance, so the model's per-limb albedo spread
    # (0.30 on the trunk to 0.50 on the knob) survives the bleach instead of
    # collapsing into one grey. That spread is load-bearing on `driftwood`: an
    # earlier revision lost a limb to shadow for exactly this reason.
    %desaturated = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionDesaturation`(Input: %vertexColor, Fraction: %bleachLocal) @(-1300, 300)
    %silvered = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %desaturated, B: %silverTint) @(-1100, 340)
    %tone = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionLinearInterpolate`(A: %desaturated, B: %silvered, Alpha: %bleachLocal) @(-900, 300)

    %grainLow = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionSubtract`(A: %one, B: %grainContrast) @(-1300, 400)
    %grainHigh = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionAdd`(A: %one, B: %grainContrast) @(-1300, 480)
    %grainMul = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionLinearInterpolate`(A: %grainLow, B: %grainHigh, Alpha: %grain) @(-1100, 440)

    %tonedGrain = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %tone, B: %grainMul) @(-700, 340)
    %baseColor = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionMultiply`(A: %tonedGrain, B: %checkMul) @(-500, 340)

    # ---- roughness varies with the grain, and peaks inside a check ---------
    # RoughnessMin below about 0.7 puts a wet sheen on the fibre crests that the
    # normal creates - measured at 0.68, the trunk came back with white highlights
    # reading as wet plastic. The floor is doing real work; do not lower it to
    # "make the wood catch more light".
    %roughGrain = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionLinearInterpolate`(A: %roughnessMin, B: %roughnessMax, Alpha: %grain) @(-1100, 900)
    %roughness = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionLinearInterpolate`(A: %roughGrain, B: %roughnessMax, Alpha: %check) @(-900, 900)

    output BaseColor: %baseColor
    output Normal: %normal
    output Roughness: %roughness
    output Metallic: %metallic
    output Specular: %specular
}

# ###########################################################################
# THE WOOD TABLE. One master, one instance per wood; nothing below needs a second
# graph. `create_material_instance` takes the whole set inline, one recompile for
# the batch, exactly as the six surface-family instances in
# `model.vertex-color.md` do. Note the nesting - the parameter sets go inside a
# `parameters` object, and a bare `scalar:` at the top level is refused:
#
#   call("material.authoring.create_material_instance", {
#     assetPath: "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/MI_PwModelExample_Oak",
#     parentMaterial: "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/M_PwModelExample_WeatheredWood",
#     parameters: { scalar: { Bleach: 0.10, GrainAcross: 9, GrainContrast: 0.35,
#                             CheckDepth: 0.25, RoughnessMin: 0.45,
#                             RoughnessMax: 0.70, Specular: 0.40 } } })
#
# | Wood         | Bleach | GrainAcross | GrainContrast | CheckDepth | Rough min/max | Specular |
# |--------------|--------|-------------|---------------|------------|---------------|----------|
# | driftwood    | 0.85   | 13          | 0.40          | 0.55       | 0.76 / 0.98   | 0.22     |
# | oak          | 0.10   |  9          | 0.35          | 0.25       | 0.45 / 0.70   | 0.40     |
# | boxwood      | 0.00   | 26          | 0.14          | 0.00       | 0.28 / 0.42   | 0.55     |
# | rough timber | 0.35   |  7          | 0.30          | 0.40       | 0.60 / 0.88   | 0.30     |
#
# Reading the table. `Bleach` is the whole silvering axis and is the first thing
# to move; at 0 the graph is a grain-and-roughness pass over the model's own
# vertex colour and nothing else. `GrainAcross` is fibres per turn - boxwood is
# the closest-grained wood in the corpus and wants roughly twice driftwood's, oak
# is coarser, sawn timber coarser again. `CheckDepth: 0` removes the splits
# entirely, which is what an interior turned or planed piece wants - a
# `chess_rook` with sea-splits in it would be nonsense.
#
# ONE NUMBER IN THAT TABLE IS NOT PORTABLE AND IT IS NOT IN THE TABLE.
# `GrainAlong` and `CheckAlong` are in 1/UV, and V's scale comes from the model's
# own `uv ... scale=`. A consumer whose `.pwmodel` unwraps at a different scale
# must re-derive both:
#
#       GrainAlong = uvScale / streakLengthInWorldUnits
#
# driftwood: 0.02 / 28.6 = 0.0007. A model unwrapped at `scale=1` needs 0.035 for
# the same streak - fifty times driftwood's. Copy driftwood's number onto it and
# every streak is fifty times too long, which renders as a limb with no
# along-length variation at all: uniform fibre from end to end, which is a
# brushed-metal read, not a wood one.
# ###########################################################################
